  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-25T19:41:32.200Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-25T19:41:32.200Z",
  "body": [
    "The U.S. economy grew at a 4.3% annualized pace from July through September, faster than first estimated and the strongest pace in three years. A separate federal report released Wednesday showed why a strong quarter can still feel expensive at the checkout. In October, consumer prices were 3.8% higher than a year earlier, and spending barely changed after adjusting for inflation.",
    "What drove the stronger growth number",
    "The Bureau of Economic Analysis raised its estimate of third-quarter growth from 3.4% to 4.3%, largely because investment and consumer spending were stronger than it first calculated. The agency cited software as an important part of the investment revision. The economy expanded about 1.1% during the quarter itself. The 4.3% figure is the annual rate the economy would reach if that quarterly pace continued for a year.",
    "GDP measures the value of what the country produces, adjusted for inflation. A rise in business investment can lift that national figure even when individual households face higher costs for the things they buy.",
    "Americans spent more in October. How much did that buy?",
    "Consumer spending rose 0.3% in dollar terms from September to October. The price index for personal consumption expenditures, known as PCE, also rose 0.3%. Spending adjusted for inflation edged down by less than 0.1%.",
    "Inflation excluding food and energy rose to 3.3% from a year earlier, up from 3.1% in September. Reuters reported that food and household furnishings were among the goods that got more expensive in October, along with services including dining out and hotel stays. The national index reflects a broad mix of purchases, so the change in a household’s own bills depends on what it buys.",
    "What to watch next",
    "October’s price figures cover the month before the Trump administration’s November 25 tariff proposal. The next household spending report will offer an early look at whether consumers began buying more again as the holiday shopping season got underway. The BEA’s next update to third-quarter GDP is due December 23."
  ],
